Z||000000||burdell1||02-04-2000||12:27 PM||||I was going to put in a standard third brake light in my '85 GT, however, I thought a longer, thinner brake light would look really cool right above the rear window (on the outside of the car.) Has anyone done this before? Is there any place that sells them cheap?||||reg||5
Z||000001||lawrence||02-04-2000||12:42 PM||||I was thinking about replacing my 3rd light because it blocks out much of the rear view, but I haven't found anything good to use.  I think I'd stay away from aftermarket LEDs, though.  If it isn't OEM, it's likely to be crap.<P>JCW has a "night rider" light fixture that I could probably convert to brake lights and mount outside the rear window, under the roof.  It's just about the correct size for that, but it isn't made very well.<P>Good luck,<P>-L||||reg||1
Z||000003||burdell1||02-08-2000||03:27 PM||||Any other ideas anyone?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-08-2000||04:29 PM||||I've yet to see good aftermarket third lights.<P>I don't even notice the factory one in my 87 unless I turn around and look up. It doesn't bother the mirror in any signifigant way.<P>Not many people care but those lights do have DOT requirements for size and brightness. Some aftermarket ones meet them some don't. Most aftermarket ones are too dim. The better ones will meet the DOT requirements and should show up in daylight.||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||02-08-2000||06:09 PM||||I too had been thinking of modifying my 3rd light in my 87 and adding one to my 85.  I have been looking at the later model (From Alante revisions up) to see if I could use those LED's easily.  So far, I haven't been able to find one in a bone yard so I don't know anything.  Mounting them behind the roof sounds like a good idea though and much easier than glassing a pocket in the decklid like I had been thinking of.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
